  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+4}-2}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+4-4}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
